.Dv KVM_VCPU_TSC_OFFSET .
.El
.Pp
.Dv KVM_CHECK_EXTENSION
answers 1, or a count, for each capability the kernel makes true, and 0
for the rest.
The backends offer
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_ROBUST_SINGLESTEP
for their single stepping and event injection.
.Dv KVM_SET_VAPIC_ADDR
accepts address zero with a local APIC in the kernel, to disable the optional
shared VAPIC page; the kernel does not offer
.Dv KVM_CAP_VAPIC .
.Pp
The bounds are Linux's:
.Dv KVM_CAP_MAX_VCPUS
is 1024 and
.Dv KVM_CAP_MAX_VCPU_ID
4096, which
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_MAX_VCPU_ID
on a VM lowers, once and before its first vCPU.
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
on a VM also takes
.Dv KVM_CAP_EXIT_ON_EMULATION_FAILURE ;
.Dv KVM_CAP_MSR_PLATFORM_INFO ,
whose argument 0 makes the guest's read of
.Dv MSR_PLATFORM_INFO
#GP;
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_USER_SPACE_MSR
and
.Dv KVM_CAP_X2APIC_API ,
below; and
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS ,
the length of the APIC timer's tick, 1 ns unless set, with the local APIC
in the kernel and before the first vCPU.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_CAP_DISABLE_QUIRKS2
names the quirks that
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of it, or of
.Dv KVM_CAP_DISABLE_QUIRKS ,
turns off; one turned off stays off, as in Linux 6.12.
They are Linux's:
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_LINT0_REENABLED ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_CD_NW_CLEARED
on AMD processors,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_LAPIC_MMIO_HOLE ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_OUT_7E_INC_RIP ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_MISC_ENABLE_NO_MWAIT ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_FIX_HYPERCALL_INSN ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_MWAIT_NEVER_UD_FAULTS ,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_STUFF_FEATURE_MSRS
and, on Intel processors that snoop their own caches,
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_IGNORE_GUEST_PAT .
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_SLOT_ZAP_ALL
and
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_VMCS12_ALLOW_FREEZE_IN_SMM
are taken and change nothing a guest can see.
.Pp
A vCPU's descriptor maps
.Dv KVM_GET_VCPU_MMAP_SIZE
bytes, three pages: the
.Vt struct kvm_run
page, the page port I/O data goes through, at
.Dv KVM_PIO_PAGE_OFFSET ,
and the VM's ring of coalesced writes, at
.Dv KVM_COALESCED_MMIO_PAGE_OFFSET ,
which every vCPU of the VM maps.
A shared mapping is the kernel's own pages, which
.Dv KVM_RUN
reads and writes, and a fork's child shares it.
A private mapping gives each page as it was when first touched, as a
private mapping of a file does, and its writes stay its own.
A page past the three raises
.Dv SIGBUS .
.Pp
A VM belongs to the address space that made it.
Its ioctls, and those of its vCPUs, made from another, as a fork's child
would make them, fail with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
.Dv KVM_RUN
gives the guest the answer to the last exit's port or MMIO read first,
then returns
.Er EINTR
at once when
.Va immediate_exit
is set in the run page, and also when a signal is pending or another
thread asked the vCPU to stop, with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_INTR
in
.Va exit_reason .
That
.Er EINTR
is never restarted, with or without
.Dv SA_RESTART ,
as on Linux.
An OUT that ends the run with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_IO
leaves RIP at the instruction, and the next
.Dv KVM_RUN
steps past it unless the caller moved RIP meanwhile, as on Linux; a string
OUT, and an OUT to port 0x7e under its quirk, are stepped before the exit.
With
.Dv KVM_CAP_SYNC_REGS ,
which names the registers, the special registers and the events,
.Dv KVM_RUN
takes from the run page those that
.Va kvm_dirty_regs
names and writes to it afterwards those that
.Va kvm_valid_regs
names.
Guests run under VMX on Intel processors and SVM with nested paging on AMD processors.
.Ss Memory
.Dv KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION ,
or
.Dv KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION2 ,
whose larger structure begins with the same members and whose flag
.Dv KVM_MEM_GUEST_MEMFD
is
.Er EINVAL ,
gives the guest a slot: the
.Va memory_size
bytes of the caller's memory from
.Va userspace_addr
at the guest-physical address
.Va guest_phys_addr ,
all three page-aligned, numbered
.Va slot ,
below 256, in address space 0.
A size of 0 deletes the slot.
A slot may move to another guest address or change its
.Dv KVM_MEM_LOG_DIRTY_PAGES
flag, and keeps its address in the caller, its size and its
.Dv KVM_MEM_READONLY
flag.
No two slots share a guest address, but two may map the same memory of
the caller.
A guest's write to a
.Dv KVM_MEM_READONLY
slot, and any access where no slot is, ends
.Dv KVM_RUN
with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_MMIO .
A slot changes while vCPUs run: what the guest reached of one that moves,
changes its flags or goes is taken from it before the call returns.
.Pp
Nothing is pinned.
The guest reaches each page through the frame the caller's page has at
the guest's first touch, brought in as the caller's own touch would bring
it, and follows the caller's memory: a copy-on-write break, a fork,
.Xr mprotect 2 ,
.Xr madvise 2 ,
.Xr munmap 2 ,
.Xr mremap 2
and the truncation of a file or device object behind a slot take the frame
from the guest first, with every vCPU out of the guest, and the guest's
next touch finds the page as the caller has it then.
A page of a device's memory, such as a mapping of
.Pa /dev/mem ,
is uncached unless the guest's own page attributes ask for
write-combining; the caller's RAM is write-back.
Where 2 MiB of guest addresses in one slot lie over 2 MiB of the caller's
pages that are 512 consecutive frames from a 2 MiB boundary, the guest
gets one 2 MiB page.
A guest's access to a page the caller cannot touch, outside its mappings
or under
.Dv PROT_NONE ,
ends
.Dv KVM_RUN
with
.Er EFAULT .
.Pp
A slot with
.Dv KVM_MEM_LOG_DIRTY_PAGES
logs each page the guest writes.
.Dv KVM_GET_DIRTY_LOG
copies the slot's log, a bit for each page in 64-bit words, to
.Va dirty_bitmap ,
clears it, and write-protects the pages it named, so that the next write
to each is logged.
After
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_MANUAL_DIRTY_LOG_PROTECT2
with
.Dv KVM_DIRTY_LOG_MANUAL_PROTECT_ENABLE ,
.Dv KVM_GET_DIRTY_LOG
leaves the log as it is, and
.Dv KVM_CLEAR_DIRTY_LOG
clears the bits its bitmap sets of
.Va num_pages
pages from
.Va first_page ,
a multiple of 64, and write-protects those pages; with
.Dv KVM_DIRTY_LOG_INITIALLY_SET
as well, a slot's log starts with every page set.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY
on a vCPU maps the guest-physical range
.Va gpa
and
.Va size
name, both page-aligned, as the guest's own faults would, and writes the
two back moved past what it mapped.
It returns 0 once anything was mapped; otherwise a range that touches no
slot fails with
.Er ENOENT
and a pending signal with
.Er EINTR .
.Ss Emulated instructions
The kernel carries out a guest's instruction in two cases, with the
results of Linux's emulator.
An instruction whose operand is MMIO runs whole: each read of the device
ends
.Dv KVM_RUN
with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_MMIO ,
of 8 bytes at most, and the instruction goes on with the answer at the
next
.Dv KVM_RUN .
Once the instruction has ended, its writes follow in pieces of 8 bytes at
most, split where a page ends, each piece one more
.Dv KVM_EXIT_MMIO ,
with no guest instruction run between; a 16-byte SSE store makes two.
An answered read finishes its instruction before
.Dv KVM_RUN
returns or injects an event, so the registers the caller reads are those
after the instruction.
A string instruction with a repeat prefix runs one element at a time.
The moves, arithmetic, shifts, multiplies and divides, bit tests,
exchanges, string and stack instructions, far transfers, segment and
descriptor-table instructions and SSE and MMX moves of Linux's emulator
are known.
.Pp
An instruction that raised #UD runs when it is SYSCALL, SYSENTER or SYSEXIT
in a mode where the guest's CPUID vendor has it and the processor does
not, MOVBE or RDPID when the guest's CPUID has it, or the hypercall instruction of the other vendor,
VMCALL on AMD processors and VMMCALL on Intel ones, which is rewritten in
the guest's memory as the processor's own and run again, or gets its #UD
once
.Dv KVM_X86_QUIRK_FIX_HYPERCALL_INSN
is turned off.
Any other gets its #UD.
.Pp
For an instruction the kernel does not know or cannot finish, the guest
gets #UD, and when it ran at CPL 0
.Dv KVM_RUN
also ends with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_INTERNAL_ERROR
and the suberror
.Dv KVM_INTERNAL_ERROR_EMULATION ,
whose data are Linux's: the flag
.Dv KVM_INTERNAL_ERROR_EMULATION_FLAG_INSTRUCTION_BYTES ,
the instruction's length and its 15 bytes, then five values as Linux's
.Fn get_exit_info
gives them: on Intel processors the exit reason, the exit qualification,
the IDT-vectoring information, the exit's interruption information and its
error code; on AMD processors the exit code, EXITINFO1, EXITINFO2,
EXITINTINFO and its error code.
Under
.Dv KVM_CAP_EXIT_ON_EMULATION_FAILURE
the run ends so at every privilege level, and the guest gets no #UD.
.Ss MSRs
.Dv KVM_GET_MSRS
and
.Dv KVM_SET_MSRS
are the caller's accesses, and RDMSR and WRMSR the guest's, with Linux's
rules for each.
.Dv MSR_IA32_MCG_CTL
without
.Dv MCG_CTL_P
in
.Dv MSR_IA32_MCG_CAP
reads 0 for the caller and takes its 0, and is #GP to the guest.
.Dv MSR_IA32_MCG_CAP
is set only by
.Dv KVM_X86_SETUP_MCE ;
neither the caller nor the guest may write it.
.Dv MSR_IA32_P5_MC_ADDR
and
.Dv MSR_IA32_P5_MC_TYPE
read 0 and take no write.
A bank's CTL2, from 0x280, holds 0 alone, since no
.Dv MCG_CMCI_P
is offered: the caller reads 0 and writes 0, and it is #GP to the guest.
A machine-check bank's STATUS takes any value from the caller, and from the
guest only 0, unless the guest's CPUID vendor is AMD or Hygon and
.Dv MSR_K7_HWCR
has McStatusWrEn, bit 18, set.
.Dv MSR_K7_HWCR
is kept for every vendor: a write may set bits 18 and 24, bits 3, 6 and 8
are dropped, and any other bit is refused.
.Dv MSR_IA32_POWER_CTL
is kept as written.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_X86_SET_MSR_FILTER
replaces the VM's filter of the guest's RDMSR and WRMSR:
up to 16 ranges, each a bitmap of
.Va nmsrs
MSRs from
.Va base ,
at most 0x600 bytes and read in whole 8-byte words, whose set bit allows the
accesses its
.Dv KVM_MSR_FILTER_READ
and
.Dv KVM_MSR_FILTER_WRITE
flags name.
The first range that holds the MSR and names the access decides it; an
MSR no range decides is allowed unless the filter's flags are
.Dv KVM_MSR_FILTER_DEFAULT_DENY .
The x2APIC's MSRs, 0x800 to 0x8ff, are never filtered, and the caller's
accesses never are.
A refused access is #GP to the guest.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_USER_SPACE_MSR
names the reasons a guest's RDMSR or WRMSR goes to the caller instead:
.Dv KVM_MSR_EXIT_REASON_INVAL ,
a value or an access the MSR refuses;
.Dv KVM_MSR_EXIT_REASON_UNKNOWN ,
an MSR the kernel does not have; and
.Dv KVM_MSR_EXIT_REASON_FILTER ,
an access the filter refuses.
.Dv KVM_RUN
then ends with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_X86_RDMSR
or
.Dv KVM_EXIT_X86_WRMSR ,
with the index, the reason and a write's EDX:EAX in
.Va msr .
At the next
.Dv KVM_RUN
a nonzero
.Va error
is the guest's #GP at the instruction; otherwise a read's
.Va data
goes to EDX:EAX and the guest goes on past the instruction.
.Ss Interrupts
The full and split interrupt controllers follow Linux's interfaces.
.Dv KVM_CREATE_IRQCHIP
on a VM, before its first vCPU, puts the PIC pair, the 24-pin I/O APIC
and each vCPU's local APIC in the kernel.
.Dv KVM_GET_IRQCHIP
and
.Dv KVM_SET_IRQCHIP
save and restore a controller selected by
.Va chip_id :
0 for the master PIC, 1 for the slave, and 2 for the I/O APIC.
The default GSI routes send inputs 0 through 15 to the PICs and I/O APIC,
and 16 through 23 to the I/O APIC alone.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_SPLIT_IRQCHIP
instead keeps the PIC, I/O APIC and PIT in the caller.
It must precede the first vCPU, with the caller's I/O APIC pin count,
at most 4096, in
.Va args[0] .
QEMU's default
.Cm kernel-irqchip=on
uses the full controller;
.Cm kernel-irqchip=split
uses the split one, and
.Cm kernel-irqchip=off
keeps the local APIC in QEMU too.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_CREATE_PIT
or
.Dv KVM_CREATE_PIT2
after the full controller creates the PIT's three counters.
The latter takes
.Dv KVM_PIT_SPEAKER_DUMMY
to add the silent speaker port; the older call always adds it.
.Dv KVM_GET_PIT2
and
.Dv KVM_SET_PIT2
save and restore the counters and flags;
.Dv KVM_PIT_FLAGS_HPET_LEGACY
stops IRQ0.
The older
.Dv KVM_GET_PIT
and
.Dv KVM_SET_PIT
take the counters alone.
By default elapsed ticks wait for acknowledgement before reinjection.
.Dv KVM_REINJECT_CONTROL
with
.Va pit_reinject
zero instead coalesces missed ticks.
.Pp
The local APIC is Linux's: its registers at the guest's APIC base and, in
x2APIC mode, in MSRs 0x800 to 0x8ff; its timer, one-shot, periodic or at
.Dv IA32_TSC_DEADLINE ,
counting at a bus of 1 GHz; the delivery of fixed, lowest-priority, NMI,
INIT and SIPI messages to the destinations Linux matches; and HLT, which
waits in the kernel for an interrupt the vCPU can take.
The guest's CPUID offers x2APIC, and the TSC-deadline mode when
.Dv KVM_CAP_TSC_DEADLINE_TIMER
is 1.
The boot vCPU's LINT0 takes ExtINT at reset, so a vector given by
.Dv KVM_INTERRUPT ,
the caller's PIC's, reaches the guest with the split controller.
With the full controller the PIC supplies the vector and
.Dv KVM_INTERRUPT
is refused.
.Dv KVM_GET_LAPIC
and
.Dv KVM_SET_LAPIC
take the APIC's 1 KiB page in Linux's layout, with the x2APIC id in bits
31:24, and in x2APIC mode
.Dv KVM_SET_LAPIC
ignores the caller's id.
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_X2APIC_API
sets its flags, which stay set.
With
.Dv KVM_X2APIC_API_USE_32BIT_IDS
the x2APIC id is whole in bits 31:0,
.Dv KVM_SET_LAPIC
refuses an x2APIC id other than the vCPU's, and an MSI's destination takes
bits 31:8 from its high address, whose bits 7:0 make it invalid.
A destination of 0xff from an MSI or an IOAPIC is a broadcast to an APIC in
x2APIC mode until
.Dv KVM_X2APIC_API_DISABLE_BROADCAST_QUIRK
makes it the x2APIC id 0xff.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_SET_GSI_ROUTING
replaces the whole routing table: one MSI or Hyper-V
.Dv KVM_IRQ_ROUTING_HV_SINT
route per GSI, or, with the full controller,
.Dv KVM_IRQ_ROUTING_IRQCHIP
routes to at most one pin of each controller per GSI.
.Dv KVM_IRQ_LINE
sets a GSI's source level on each of its routes, and
.Dv KVM_IRQ_LINE_STATUS
also gives in
.Va irq
the count of destinations that accepted it, zero when coalesced, or \-1;
.Dv KVM_SIGNAL_MSI
sends an MSI and returns that count.
With the split controller, when a vector raised level-triggered through
the route of a GSI below the I/O APIC's pin count ends with an EOI,
.Dv KVM_RUN
returns with
.Dv KVM_EXIT_IOAPIC_EOI
and the vector in
.Va eoi.vector .
With the full controller the EOI reaches the I/O APIC inside the kernel;
an asserted level is delivered again after remote IRR is cleared.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_IRQFD
ties an eventfd, see
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
to a GSI: each write that raises its counter raises the GSI and
takes the counter.
The tie ends with
.Dv KVM_IRQFD_FLAG_DEASSIGN ,
the VM's end or the eventfd's last close.
.Dv KVM_IRQFD_FLAG_RESAMPLE
with the full controller holds the line until acknowledgement, then
lowers its source and signals the eventfd in
.Va resamplefd .
Other sources holding the same line remain asserted.
The split controller refuses resampling, as Linux does, and QEMU
resamples in its own I/O APIC.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_IOEVENTFD
ties an eventfd to a port, with
.Dv KVM_IOEVENTFD_FLAG_PIO ,
or to an MMIO address: a guest's write there of
.Va len
bytes, or of any length when
.Va len
is 0, and of
.Va datamatch
with
.Dv KVM_IOEVENTFD_FLAG_DATAMATCH ,
adds 1 to the counter and goes no further.
A string OUT adds 1 for each element.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_REGISTER_COALESCED_MMIO
names a zone of MMIO addresses or, with
.Va pio
1, of ports
.Pq Dv KVM_CAP_COALESCED_MMIO , KVM_CAP_COALESCED_PIO .
A guest's write of 8 bytes at most that lies whole inside a zone is put on
the VM's ring, Linux's
.Vt struct kvm_coalesced_mmio_ring
of 170 entries, and the guest runs on; the caller takes the entries from
.Va first
to
.Va last
at its next exit and moves
.Va first .
A write that finds the ring full ends the run as any other.
.Dv KVM_UNREGISTER_COALESCED_MMIO
removes each zone that holds its range.
A bus holds 1000 zones; ioeventfds have no limit of their own, but none is added to a bus that holds 1000 zones.
.Ss Statistics
.Dv KVM_GET_STATS_FD
on a VM or a vCPU
.Pq Dv KVM_CAP_BINARY_STATS_FD
gives a read-only descriptor,
.Pa anon_inode:kvm-vm-stats
or
.Pa anon_inode:kvm-vcpu-stats:N ,
of Linux's binary statistics, which
.Fn pread ,
see
.Xr read 2 ,
reads at any offset: the header, the id
.Ql kvm-PID
or
.Ql kvm-PID/vcpu-N ,
the descriptors of Linux 6.12's statistics in its order and their values.
A vCPU counts
.Va exits ,
.Va io_exits ,
.Va mmio_exits ,
.Va halt_exits ,
.Va irq_window_exits ,
.Va signal_exits ,
.Va hypercalls ,
.Va insn_emulation ,
.Va insn_emulation_fail ,
.Va preemption_reported
and
.Va preemption_other ,
and a VM
.Va pages_4k ,
.Va pages_2m ,
.Va remote_tlb_flush
and
.Va remote_tlb_flush_requests ;
the rest read 0.
The descriptor keeps its VM alive.
.Ss Hypercalls
A VMCALL, or VMMCALL on AMD, that is not Hyper-V's is one of Linux's KVM
hypercalls: the number in RAX, the arguments in RBX, RCX, RDX and RSI, and
the result in RAX.
.Dv KVM_HC_VAPIC_POLL_IRQ
and
.Dv KVM_HC_SCHED_YIELD
return 0.
.Dv KVM_HC_KICK_CPU
wakes the halted vCPU whose APIC id is in RCX, whatever its interrupt flag.
.Dv KVM_HC_SEND_IPI ,
with the local APIC in the kernel, sends the interrupt command in RSI to the
vCPUs that the bitmap in RBX and RCX names, bit 0 the APIC id in RDX, and
returns the number that took it.
.Dv KVM_HC_CLOCK_PAIRING
writes at the guest-physical address in RBX the real time and the guest's
TSC read together, for the clock type
.Dv KVM_CLOCK_PAIRING_WALLCLOCK
in RCX while the kernel's clock reads the TSC, and returns 0, or
.Er -KVM_EOPNOTSUPP .
Any other number returns
.Er -KVM_ENOSYS ,
and a call from outside the guest's kernel
.Er -KVM_EPERM .
Under
.Dv KVM_CAP_ENFORCE_PV_FEATURE_CPUID
a hypercall whose feature the vCPU's CPUID does not name returns
.Er -KVM_ENOSYS .
.Ss The kvmclock
.Dv KVM_GET_SUPPORTED_CPUID
gives KVM's leaves: 0x40000000, with the signature
.Ql KVMKVMKVM
and 0x40000001 in EAX, and 0x40000001, whose EAX names
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_NOP_IO_DELAY ,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_STEAL_TIME ,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_PV_UNHALT ,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_PV_SEND_IPI ,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_POLL_CONTROL
and
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_PV_SCHED_YIELD ,
and, once the kernel has measured the TSC's rate,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_CLOCKSOURCE ,
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_CLOCKSOURCE2
and
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_CLOCKSOURCE_STABLE_BIT .
.Dv KVM_SET_CPUID2
finds them at any base from 0x40000000 to 0x4000ff00 in steps of 0x100,
and drops
.Dv KVM_FEATURE_PV_UNHALT
while the VM has
.Dv KVM_X86_DISABLE_EXITS_HLT .
.Pp
A guest's write of
.Dv MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME_NEW
or
.Dv MSR_KVM_SYSTEM_TIME
names the
.Vt struct pvclock_vcpu_time_info
the kernel keeps in its memory, and of
.Dv MSR_KVM_WALL_CLOCK_NEW
or
.Dv MSR_KVM_WALL_CLOCK
the
.Vt struct pvclock_wall_clock
it writes once.
The kvmclock is the host's monotonic clock plus the VM's offset, 0 when the
VM is made.
While the kernel's clock reads the TSC, every vCPU's TSC is matched and
the boot vCPU's guest uses the new MSR, the VM has Linux's master clock:
every vCPU's copy is taken at one moment and has
.Dv PVCLOCK_TSC_STABLE_BIT ,
and
.Dv KVM_GET_CLOCK
sets
.Dv KVM_CLOCK_TSC_STABLE ,
.Dv KVM_CLOCK_REALTIME
and
.Dv KVM_CLOCK_HOST_TSC
with the real time and the host's TSC at
.Va clock .
Otherwise its flags are 0.
A vCPU made, and a TSC that
.Dv KVM_SET_MSRS
writes within a second of the one the last write would read now, keeps
the matched TSC.
.Dv KVM_GET_DEVICE_ATTR
of
.Dv KVM_VCPU_TSC_OFFSET
in group
.Dv KVM_VCPU_TSC_CTRL ,
which
.Dv KVM_CAP_VCPU_ATTRIBUTES
offers, reads the vCPU's TSC offset, the guest's TSC less the host's, into
the
.Vt u64
at
.Va addr ,
and
.Dv KVM_SET_DEVICE_ATTR
sets it, so that the guest's TSC reads the host's, scaled to the vCPU's
rate, plus the offset; the same offset set on vCPUs of the same rate keeps
the TSC matched.
.Dv KVM_HAS_DEVICE_ATTR
answers 0 for it.
.Pp
A guest's write of
.Dv MSR_KVM_STEAL_TIME ,
which
.Dv KVM_CAP_STEAL_TIME
offers, names a
.Vt struct kvm_steal_time
of 64 bytes at a 64-byte boundary in its memory, with bit 0 to turn it on;
a write with any of bits 1 to 5 fails.
Before the guest runs after it was switched out, and at each
.Dv KVM_RUN ,
the kernel adds to
.Va steal
the time the vCPU's thread waited for a processor since the last time,
which
.Pa /proc/PID/schedstat
gives as its second field
.Pq Xr proc 5 ,
with
.Va version
odd while it writes and even after.
When the thread gives up the processor to another thread at the end of its
time slice after the guest stopped for a host interrupt,
.Va preempted
says
.Dv KVM_VCPU_PREEMPTED
until the guest runs again.
A guest's INIT turns the steal time and the kvmclock off.
.Dv KVM_KVMCLOCK_CTRL
sets
.Dv PVCLOCK_GUEST_STOPPED
in the vCPU's next copy, or fails with
.Er EINVAL
when its guest has no kvmclock.
Under
.Dv KVM_CAP_ENFORCE_PV_FEATURE_CPUID ,
which
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
sets on a vCPU, the guest's access to an MSR of a feature its CPUID does
not name raises #GP, or goes to the caller as an MSR KVM does not have; the
caller's
.Dv KVM_GET_MSRS
reads it as 0, and
.Dv KVM_SET_MSRS
takes a write of 0.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_DISABLE_EXITS
on a VM takes
.Dv KVM_X86_DISABLE_EXITS_HLT
and
.Dv KVM_X86_DISABLE_EXITS_PAUSE ,
which
.Dv KVM_CHECK_EXTENSION
names, at any time; a vCPU made afterwards runs HLT in the guest.
.Ss The TSC's rate
.Dv KVM_SET_TSC_KHZ
on a vCPU sets the rate of its TSC in kHz, 0 for the host's, and on a VM
the rate its later vCPUs start at;
.Dv KVM_GET_TSC_KHZ
reads either back, the host's rate until one is set.
A rate within 250 parts in a million of the host's runs at the host's.
Another is scaled by the processor, AMD's TSC ratio MSR or Intel's TSC
multiplier, where it has one, which
.Dv KVM_CAP_TSC_CONTROL
and
.Dv KVM_CAP_VM_TSC_CONTROL
report; the guest's kvmclock, its APIC's TSC deadline and Hyper-V's
reference TSC then count at that rate.
A rate at or above what the largest ratio gives, 2^31 \- 1 kHz at most, is
.Er EINVAL .
On a processor that scales nothing, as on Linux, a rate below the host's is
.Er EINVAL ,
and a rate above it is kept by moving the guest's TSC forward before each
entry.
.Ss Hyper-V
A vCPU whose CPUID, given by
.Dv KVM_SET_CPUID2 ,
has the interface
.Ql Hv#1
in leaf 0x40000001 gets Linux's Hyper-V enlightenments.
.Dv KVM_GET_SUPPORTED_HV_CPUID ,
on
.Pa /dev/kvm
or on a vCPU, gives the ten leaves Linux gives, from 0x40000000 to
0x4000000a, with no enlightened VMCS; on a vCPU of a VM without a local APIC in the
kernel they offer no direct timers and no cluster IPI.
The guest's MSRs from 0x40000000 are Hyper-V's: its id, the hypercall page,
the VP index, the runtime, the reference counter and the reference TSC
page, the TSC and APIC frequencies, the APIC's EOI, ICR and TPR, the VP
assist page, the crash and reset MSRs, the TSC emulation and invariant TSC
controls, SynIC's, the synthetic timers' and the synthetic debugger's.
An MSR the guest's CPUID does not offer is #GP under
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_ENFORCE_CPUID ,
which
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
on the vCPU turns on.
A write of the crash control's notice bit, and of 1 to the reset MSR, ends
the next
.Dv KVM_RUN
in
.Dv KVM_EXIT_SYSTEM_EVENT
of type
.Dv KVM_SYSTEM_EVENT_CRASH
or
.Dv KVM_SYSTEM_EVENT_RESET .
.Pp
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_SYNIC
or
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_SYNIC2 ,
the second leaving the pages the guest enables as they are, turns SynIC on
for the vCPU, with a local APIC in the kernel.
A guest's write of SCONTROL, SIEFP or SIMP ends the run in
.Dv KVM_EXIT_HYPERV
of type
.Dv KVM_EXIT_HYPERV_SYNIC .
A route of type
.Dv KVM_IRQ_ROUTING_HV_SINT
raises the SINT
.Va sint
of the vCPU whose VP index is
.Va vcpu .
The four synthetic timers of each vCPU deliver their messages to the
message page, or their vector in direct mode.
.Pp
A VMCALL, or VMMCALL on AMD, from the guest's kernel, once the guest has
written its id, is a hypercall.
The kernel carries out HvNotifyLongSpinWait, HvSignalEvent for a connection
that
.Dv KVM_HYPERV_EVENTFD
ties to an eventfd, HvCallSendSyntheticClusterIpi, HvFlushVirtualAddressSpace
and HvFlushVirtualAddressList, the last three in their Ex forms too.
HvPostMessage, HvSignalEvent for another connection, the extended
hypercalls and the debugger's end the run in
.Dv KVM_EXIT_HYPERV
of type
.Dv KVM_EXIT_HYPERV_HCALL ;
the caller puts the status in
.Va hyperv.u.hcall.result ,
which the next
.Dv KVM_RUN
gives the guest.
A guest's write of the debugger's control or pending page ends the run in
.Dv KVM_EXIT_HYPERV_SYNDBG ,
whose
.Va status
the next
.Dv KVM_RUN
takes.
.Ss Performance counters
The guest PMU follows the host: Intel architectural version 2 or earlier,
with general and fixed counters; AMD legacy counters, the core-extension
aliases and, where present, PerfMonV2 global control and status.
CPUID and the MSR index lists describe the registers available.
RDPMC requires CPL 0 or CR4.PCE.
Counter overflow sets global status and queues a virtual local APIC
performance interrupt through LVTPC after the next guest exit.
A guest's counters run on the physical counters the host's
hard-lockup detector does not hold, each on one its event may use by
Linux's event constraints for the processor's model, and a guest fixed
counter may run on a general counter with the fixed counter's event;
one left without a counter does not count.
Intel's PERF_CAPABILITIES offers full-width counter writes and, on a
processor with a legacy LBR stack of format 4 or later, the host's LBR
format.
A guest with that format, CPUID.PDCM and the host's model has the
host's LBR stack: IA32_DEBUGCTL's LBR and FREEZE_LBRS_ON_PMI, LBR_SELECT,
LBR_TOS and each record's FROM, TO and, from Skylake, INFO registers.
The caller writes PERF_CAPABILITIES with no LBR format but 0 or the
host's.
IA32_DEBUGCTL also keeps BUS_LOCK_DETECT and RTM_DEBUG where the host
has them and the guest's CPUID offers them, and
.Dv KVM_GET_MSR_INDEX_LIST
lists it on both vendors.
On AMD, IA32_DEBUGCTL reads 0 and takes and ignores any write, and
LASTBRANCHFROMIP, LASTBRANCHTOIP, LASTINTFROMIP and LASTINTTOIP are
listed too, read 0 and take only the caller's write of 0.
PEBS is not advertised.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_CAP_PMU_EVENT_FILTER
and
.Dv KVM_CAP_PMU_EVENT_MASKED_EVENTS
advertise
.Dv KVM_SET_PMU_EVENT_FILTER
on a VM.
Its Linux structure selects ALLOW or DENY for up to 300 event entries,
a separate fixed-counter bitmap and optional masked unit matches.
An exclusion entry overrides an inclusion.
A replacement takes effect when each running vCPU next leaves the guest.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_PMU_CAPABILITY
accepts
.Dv KVM_PMU_CAP_DISABLE
in args[0] before any vCPU has been created.
An unsupported bit, or a request after creating a vCPU, is EINVAL.
.Pp
.Pa /sys/module/kvm/parameters/enable_pmu
reads Y or N according to the host PMU probe.
.Pa /sys/module/kvm/parameters/force_emulation_prefix
reads 0.
Both are read-only.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /sys/module/kvm/parameters
.It Pa /dev/kvm
the interface
.It Pa /sys/module/kvm/parameters
the parameters Linux's KVM selftests read, and those of
.Pa kvm_intel
or
.Pa kvm_amd
beside it; see
.Xr sysfs 5
.El
.Ss VFIO assignment
.Dv KVM_CAP_DEVICE_CTRL
reports the device-control API.
.Dv KVM_CREATE_DEVICE
accepts
.Dv KVM_DEV_TYPE_VFIO
and returns a close-on-exec descriptor in the request's
.Va fd
member.
Only one such bridge exists per VM; another creation is
.Er EBUSY .
.Dv KVM_CREATE_DEVICE_TEST
checks the type without creating it.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_SET_DEVICE_ATTR
of group
.Dv KVM_DEV_VFIO_FILE
accepts
.Dv KVM_DEV_VFIO_FILE_ADD
and
.Dv KVM_DEV_VFIO_FILE_DEL ,
with
.Va addr
pointing to a signed 32-bit VFIO group or device descriptor.
ADD holds the open file description; adding an alias of a held file is
.Er EEXIST .
DEL releases that reference, or returns
.Er ENOENT
if the file is absent.
The bridge's last close releases every remaining file and its VM reference.
While files are assigned, KVM applies the guest PAT to RAM translations
because VFIO does not enforce DMA cache coherence.
.Pp
.Dv KVM_HAS_DEVICE_ATTR
reports these two attributes.
An unknown attribute is
.Er ENXIO ;
.Dv KVM_GET_DEVICE_ATTR
is
.Er EPERM .
Device ioctls from another address space are
.Er EIO .
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er ENOTTY
An ioctl that
.Pa /dev/kvm
or a VM does not take;
.Dv KVM_ENABLE_CAP
of
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_ENLIGHTENED_VMCS
or
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_DIRECT_TLBFLUSH ,
which serve a nested guest.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An ioctl that a vCPU does not take; an argument outside Linux's bounds,
such as a machine type other than 0, a vCPU id of 4096 or more or past
.Dv KVM_CAP_MAX_VCPU_ID ,
a TSS past
4 GiB less three pages, register state the processor would refuse, an
XSAVE image past the guest's XCR0, or a flag Linux does not define;
.Dv KVM_SET_USER_MEMORY_REGION
of an address or size not page-aligned, a range past user space or the
host's physical width, the deletion of no slot, or a change of a slot's
address in the caller, size or
.Dv KVM_MEM_READONLY
flag;
.Dv KVM_CLEAR_DIRTY_LOG
of a range outside the slot or not on 64-page bounds;
.Dv KVM_SET_TSC_KHZ
of a rate the processor cannot give the guest;
.Dv KVM_PRE_FAULT_MEMORY
with flags or a range that is empty, wraps or is not page-aligned;
.Dv KVM_REGISTER_COALESCED_MMIO
with a
.Va pio
above 1;
.Dv KVM_CAP_DISABLE_QUIRKS2
of a quirk it does not name;
.Dv KVM_CAP_MAX_VCPU_ID
a second time with another bound, above 4096 or below the boot vCPU's id;
.Dv KVM_CAP_X86_APIC_BUS_CYCLES_NS
of 0, of a length at which the longest timer count, 2^32 - 1 at the divisor
128, overflows 64 bits of ns, or once the VM has a vCPU;
synchronized registers
.Dv KVM_CAP_SYNC_REGS
does not name;
.Dv KVM_CAP_HYPERV_SYNIC
without a local APIC in the kernel;
.Dv KVM_HYPERV_EVENTFD
of a connection past 0xffffff;
.Dv KVM_X86_SET_MSR_FILTER
of a flag it does not name, a range of MSRs with no flag, a bitmap past
